WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.590
<v Instructor>Okay, so now let's discuss</v>

2
00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:04.290
Identity Federation in AWS.

3
00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:06.630
So the idea is that you want to give users

4
00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:09.420
that sit outside of AWS permissions to access

5
00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.730
your AWS resources in your accounts.

6
00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:14.160
And the idea is that because the users already exist

7
00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.500
within your corporate directory,

8
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.680
then you don't want to create specific IAM users for them

9
00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.260
because you'd like to do user management outside of AWS.

10
00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:24.540
So this is why we need Identity Federation.

11
00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.280
So the use cases is, for example,

12
00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:28.560
your corporation has its own identity system,

13
00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.270
for example, Active Directory,

14
00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:31.860
or you have a web and mobile application

15
00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.410
that needs to access AWS resources.

16
00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:36.390
And so this is where we need Identity Federation.

17
00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:38.490
So at its core, here's what it looks like.

18
00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:41.430
So the user wants to access AWS,

19
00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:43.440
and we have an identity provider.

20
00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:45.030
First, and this will not be represented

21
00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:48.240
on any following graphs, but just on this one,

22
00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:49.380
obviously you need to set up

23
00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:51.870
a trust relationship between AWS

24
00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:55.320
and the identity provider to tell AWS that it's fine

25
00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.530
to get an identity from this one provider.

26
00:00:58.530 --> 00:00:59.880
Then the user will perform a login

27
00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:01.770
onto this identity provider,

28
00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.290
will receive back credentials for AWS,

29
00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:08.820
and will access AWS using these temporary credentials.

30
00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:11.880
So Identity Federation can have multiple flavors.

31
00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:15.180
It could be SAML 2.0, a custom identity broker,

32
00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:18.330
Web Identity Federation with or without Amazon Cognito,

33
00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:20.790
and then IAM Identity Center.

34
00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:23.400
So I'm going to discuss the first three,

35
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.080
single sign-on will have its own lecture,

36
00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:27.540
and this is the more recent generation

37
00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:28.920
of Identity Federation,

38
00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:30.450
but still from an example perspective,

39
00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:32.490
you need to know about all of them.

40
00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:36.210
So let's go ahead with SAML 2.0 Federation.

41
00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:40.170
So SAML 2.0 stands for Security Assertion Markup Language,

42
00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:41.370
and it's an open standard used

43
00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:43.920
by many identity providers such as ADFS.

44
00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.140
It supports integration with Microsoft Active Directory,

45
00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:50.040
which is nice, or any SAML 2.0-compatible IDP,

46
00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:51.843
which is identity provider with AWS.

47
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.170
Using this, we can access the console, the CLI,

48
00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:57.600
or any API using temporary credentials.

49
00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:58.980
So we don't need to create IAM users

50
00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:01.830
for each of the employees in your ADFS.

51
00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:04.980
And you need, for example, of course to set up a trust

52
00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:09.570
between IAM and your SAML 2.0 identity providers both ways.

53
00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:11.670
So under the hood we'll use an API

54
00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:16.290
called AssumeRoleWithSAML, comes from the STS service,

55
00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:18.120
and that will give us temporary credentials

56
00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:19.980
using SAML assertion.

57
00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.060
So as I said,

58
00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.820
SAML 2.0 Federation is the old way of doing things.

59
00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:25.410
And now IAM Identity Center

60
00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:27.060
is the new managed way

61
00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.170
and simpler way of doing things,

62
00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:31.980
yet we still have to learn about SAML 2.0 Federation.

63
00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:33.390
So the best way to understand this is

64
00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:35.280
to go through a diagram.

65
00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.640
So we have our corporation where our identity provider sits,

66
00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:42.540
and AWS, and our user wants to access an S3 bucket.

67
00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:44.370
Now inside of our corporation,

68
00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:46.650
we'll have a portal or identity provider,

69
00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:50.247
and the user will make an authentication request to the IDP.

70
00:02:50.247 --> 00:02:54.510
The IDP will verify the request through an identity store,

71
00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:56.130
for example, LDAP-based,

72
00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:59.070
and then it will return a SAML assertion

73
00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:01.290
if the login is successful.

74
00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:02.760
So this SAML assertion is the proof

75
00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.250
that the user is who they claim to be,

76
00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:07.770
and therefore with this SAML assertion,

77
00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:10.650
the user can call the STS service

78
00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:13.020
with AssumeRoleWithSAML API,

79
00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:15.120
and STS will verify the assertion,

80
00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:17.880
make sure that yes, it is the one that is trusted,

81
00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:20.853
and then say, "Okay, here are some temporary

82
00:03:20.853 --> 00:03:21.960
security credentials for you."

83
00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:24.630
And the user will be able to access the AWS APIs

84
00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:26.550
using that mechanism.

85
00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:28.740
It is very similar if the user

86
00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:30.600
wants to access the console of AWS.

87
00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:31.950
So very similar setup.

88
00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:35.280
So we still have the IDP and the LDAP identity-based store.

89
00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:38.160
We have the authentication loop, as I said from before,

90
00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.740
but this time we post to AWS sign-in

91
00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:43.290
in this endpoint with slash SAML.

92
00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:44.700
And what this will return is

93
00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:47.520
that it will return security credentials

94
00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:49.260
through the STS service,

95
00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:51.000
and this will return a special URL,

96
00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.100
the sign-in URL for the AWS console.

97
00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:55.200
And this URL will be used by the user

98
00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.870
to be redirected into the AWS management console.

99
00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.760
And from there on, everything is good.

100
00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:01.590
So these two flows are very similar

101
00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:05.580
because they use the same SAML 2.0 Federation.

102
00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:08.100
Now if we have ADFS, it's something you may have.

103
00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:11.280
Well, this is the exact same mechanism as before.

104
00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.500
The only difference is that on the left hand side,

105
00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:16.800
we have the Microsoft Active Directory Federation Services

106
00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:19.440
on the top left, and then on the bottom right,

107
00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:23.070
where the user sits, is Active directory itself, okay?

108
00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:24.540
So this is for you to understand

109
00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:27.690
what is the difference between the IDP, so ADFS,

110
00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:30.723
and the directory itself, which is Active Directory.

111
00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:34.410
Okay, more complicated and definitely older

112
00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:37.350
is that if your identity provider is not compatible

113
00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:38.859
with SAML 2.0,

114
00:04:38.859 --> 00:04:41.370
then you need to use a custom identity broker.

115
00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:44.400
So let's say we want to access AWS services

116
00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:45.930
and the management console,

117
00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:48.210
and we have our corporation identity provider,

118
00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:50.580
we'll have a custom identity broker.

119
00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:53.400
Now the user will login to the identity broker,

120
00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.710
which will verify the login itself,

121
00:04:55.710 --> 00:05:00.150
but then the identity broker will be the one to authenticate

122
00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:02.690
and request temporary credentials from AWS.

123
00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:05.850
So this time, there is no AWS API that can be used.

124
00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:09.300
So therefore the custom identity broker has admin power

125
00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:11.550
and can request temporary credentials directly

126
00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:13.230
from the STS service,

127
00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:15.750
which will be returning these credentials themselves.

128
00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:19.380
Now that means that the identity broker is responsible

129
00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:23.310
and must determine the appropriate IAM role for that user.

130
00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:25.200
So it pushes the management

131
00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.380
of the users into the identity broker itself.

132
00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:31.005
Then the only way for it to work is to use the AssumeRole

133
00:05:31.005 --> 00:05:34.140
or GetFederationToken API from STS directly

134
00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:36.090
from the custom identity broker.

135
00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:38.550
So once the security credentials are retrieved,

136
00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:39.840
then they're passed on to the user,

137
00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:42.480
and the user can access the APIs of AWS

138
00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:45.750
or be redirected to the management console.

139
00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:48.120
So again, these are the old ways of doing things.

140
00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:51.360
SSO will come up later in this course.

141
00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:54.360
Now with web identity federation, we have two flavors,

142
00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:56.820
we have without Cognito and with Cognito.

143
00:05:56.820 --> 00:06:00.120
So without Cognito is not recommended by AWS anymore.

144
00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:01.830
You're recommended to use Cognito instead,

145
00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:03.960
but still you need to know about it.

146
00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:06.150
So what is Web Identity Federation?

147
00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:08.190
So this time we have an untrusted environment.

148
00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:11.280
So before, it was our corporation, so we knew the clients,

149
00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:13.590
but in this case, we have AWS Cloud,

150
00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:16.710
and we want to access services from our client directly,

151
00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:18.210
but we are going to authenticate

152
00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:20.310
with a third party identity provider.

153
00:06:20.310 --> 00:06:22.140
It could be Amazon, Google, Facebook,

154
00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:25.380
or any OpenID Connect-compatible IDP.

155
00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:27.200
So this obviously will be set up with AWS

156
00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:29.220
as a trust mechanism.

157
00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:32.190
And so the flow is that the client will log in into the IDP,

158
00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:34.050
the third party IDP,

159
00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:36.360
and then the web identity token

160
00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:38.880
is going to be shared back with the clients.

161
00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:41.700
The client will use a special STS API

162
00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:43.643
called the AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity API.

163
00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:47.280
And then by trading that token,

164
00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:48.480
it will receive

165
00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:51.930
temporary security security credentials for AWS.

166
00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:53.310
These credentials will be used

167
00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:55.950
to access AWS resources directly.

168
00:06:55.950 --> 00:06:57.693
So this is the old way without Cognito.

169
00:06:57.693 --> 00:07:00.660
With Cognito, it's a little bit more secure and more simple.

170
00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:03.660
So it's preferred over Web Identity Federation

171
00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:04.980
because here in Cognito,

172
00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:06.240
we can create IAM roles

173
00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:08.100
with the least privilege needed,

174
00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:09.450
and then we just need to build the trust

175
00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:12.480
between the OIDC IdP and AWS.

176
00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:14.070
So now we have the Cognito service,

177
00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:15.360
the client still authenticates

178
00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:19.080
to a third party identity provider, gets a token back,

179
00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:22.290
but then the token is exchanged with Amazon Cognito,

180
00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:24.510
which will return a Cognito token.

181
00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:27.150
That Cognito token can be exchanged with STS

182
00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:30.090
to receive temporary security credentials for AWS.

183
00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:31.770
And then the client will be able

184
00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:35.010
to access the AWS resources directly.

185
00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:37.710
So why should we use this mechanism with Cognito?

186
00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:40.980
Well, Cognito will support anonymous users, which is a plus.

187
00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:44.760
It will support MFA, and it will have data synchronization.

188
00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:45.960
So in that instance,

189
00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:47.970
Amazon Cognito operates at what's called

190
00:07:47.970 --> 00:07:49.800
a token vending machine

191
00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:53.130
because it exchanges token for credentials.

192
00:07:53.130 --> 00:07:55.980
Okay, so when we have Web Identity Federation,

193
00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:57.330
can we restrict IAM policies?

194
00:07:57.330 --> 00:08:00.720
The answer is yes, you can use an IAM policy variable.

195
00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:04.860
And so here is an example of a policy that allows the user

196
00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:09.860
to only list a bucket with a prefix as the user ID,

197
00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:11.970
and also to get, update,

198
00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:14.910
and put objects in with that specific prefix.

199
00:08:14.910 --> 00:08:18.450
So the idea is that when you have a Web Identity Federation,

200
00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:19.830
you can use some special variables.

201
00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:23.321
For example, going to identity.amazon.com:sub

202
00:08:23.321 --> 00:08:27.180
or amazon.com:user_id and so on

203
00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:30.150
to restrict your IAM policies with conditions

204
00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:31.890
and make sure that the users really have

205
00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:33.990
the restrictions they need, okay?

206
00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:36.450
So that's it for Identity Federation.

207
00:08:36.450 --> 00:08:37.890
These are the old ways, obviously,

208
00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:38.940
except when we're using Cognito.

209
00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:41.460
And we'll see SSO in the future lecture.

210
00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:42.390
So I hope you liked this,

211
00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:44.010
and I will strongly suggest

212
00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:46.440
you revisit this lecture one more time before the exam

213
00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:47.850
and it should all make sense,

214
00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:49.800
and I will see you in the next lecture.

